      "body": "## Proposal\n\n### What is the root cause of that problem?\n\n`inactiveVendor` currently gets its “Supplier” vs “Vendor” wording from `violation.data.isSupplierViolation`, which defaults to `false`.\n\nA violation created by the backend after a Xero sync can arrive without that flag. The App adds or reconciles the flag when violations are recalculated locally, but simply reopening the expense does not guarantee that recalculation. As a result, the Supplier row correctly derives its label from the workspace’s current Xero vendor-matching source, while the violation independently falls back to “Vendor no longer valid”.\n\nThere is also a stale-state edge case to avoid: using `isSupplierViolation || isSupplier` would fix a missing flag, but an old `isSupplierViolation: true` could still render “Supplier” after the active matching source changes back to QBO or Intacct. The existing reconciliation code already explicitly strips that stale flag when recalculation runs.\n\nSo when current policy context is available, it should be authoritative for the wording; the stored flag should only be a fallback when that context is unavailable.\n\n### What changes do you think we should make in order to solve the problem?\n\n1. Add an optional render-time `isSupplier` value to `getViolationTranslation()`.\n2. For `inactiveVendor`, prefer that current value when provided, and otherwise preserve the existing `data.isSupplierViolation` fallback. In other words, `false` from a current non-Xero source must be able to override a stale `true` flag.\n3. Pass the value derived from `isXeroActiveMatchingSource(policy)` through the rendering paths that can actually surface `inactiveVendor`:\n   - `MoneyRequestView`\n   - `TransactionPreviewContent`\n   - `TransactionItemRowRBR` / `getRBRMessages`\n4. Avoid changing receipt/billable-only translation paths that cannot render this violation.\n\nI would add regression coverage for:\n\n- backend-style `inactiveVendor` with no supplier flag + active Xero → “Supplier no longer valid”\n- the same violation + QBO/Intacct → “Vendor no longer valid”\n- stale `isSupplierViolation: true` + current non-Xero source → “Vendor no longer valid”\n- no current policy context + `isSupplierViolation: true` → preserve the existing Supplier fallback\n- the RBR rendering path uses the same terminology as the expense row\n\n### What alternative solutions did you explore? (Optional)\n\n- **Set `isSupplierViolation` on the backend.** This improves newly-created violations, but does not repair already-stored violations or view-only paths.\n- **Force violation recalculation when the expense opens.** This adds unrelated work to expense opening just to correct presentation.\n- **Use `isSupplierViolation || isSupplier`.** This fixes the missing-flag case, but does not let the current non-Xero source override stale Supplier metadata.",
